#open-question(title: [Could LDN vs Metformin Ever Be a Phenotype-Guided First-Line Choice?])[

A proposed stratification framework suggests choosing between repurposed drugs by dominant mechanism: LDN for a neuroimmune/TRPM3-dominant phenotype, metformin for an immunometabolic phenotype (insulin resistance, AMPK/mTOR dysregulation). The current evidence base does not support this as a triage rule — no head-to-head comparison exists, no validated response predictor is available for either drug, and the strongest metformin signal is *timing, not phenotype*: benefit in COVID-OUT was confined to acute-phase initiation (Long COVID incidence 6.3% vs 10.4% by day 300), while both randomized trials of metformin in established disease were negative (REVIVE metformin arm halted for futility @Reis2026fluvoxamine; a separate PASC recovery RCT found no benefit @Lim2026MetforminPASC). On the LDN side, TRPM3 NK-cell dysfunction is a reproducible abnormality restored in vitro by naltrexone @Sasso2026trpm3 @Sasso2025LDNtrpm3LC, but restoration of channel function has never been shown to predict clinical response. Metformin's immunometabolic reach (Th17/Treg balance, B-cell biology, flares in SLE/RA trials) is real but modest and inconsistent @Ursini2018MetforminAutoimmunity @Sun2020MetforminSLE @Gharib2021MetforminRA. The two drugs could also be complementary rather than competing — acting on different branches of the same process (@hyp:metabolic-immune-ldn).

*Falsifiable prediction:* A trial stratifying established ME/CFS patients by (a) functional TRPM3 NK-cell assay and (b) insulin-resistance phenotype (HOMA-IR), then randomizing within strata to LDN vs metformin, would show a phenotype × drug interaction (significantly better response to LDN in the TRPM3-abnormal/insulin-sensitive stratum and to metformin in the TRPM3-normal/insulin-resistant stratum). Falsified if response is independent of both markers — the likely outcome given that no current marker predicts response to either drug.

*Consequence:* Today, neither drug can be selected by biomarker: the honest default is mechanism-based reasoning plus careful individual dose-response probing, not phenotype triage. The question defines what a future decision aid would need — validated response predictors — and warns against importing metformin into established ME/CFS on the strength of its acute-phase prevention signal. (Severity applicability: unknown — no severity-stratified data. Origin: brainstorm/assessment.)
] <oq:ldn-metformin-phenotype-triage>
